window.DOCS_INDEX["pnpm"] = [{"u":"aliases.html","t":"Aliases","n":"pnpm","b":"Aliases let you install packages with custom names. Let's assume you use all over your project. There is a bug in that breaks your project. You have a fix but won't merge it. Normally you would either install from your fork directly (as a git hosted dependency) or publish it with a different name. If you use the second solution you have to replace all the requires in your project with the new dependency name ( = ). With aliases, you have a third option. Publish a new package called and install it using as its alias: No changes in code are needed. All the requires of will now resolve to . Somet "},{"u":"catalogs.html","t":"Catalogs","n":"pnpm","b":"\" Catalogs \" are a workspace feature for defining dependency version ranges as reusable constants. Constants defined in catalogs can later be referenced in files. <iframe width=\"560\" height=\"315\" src=\"https://www.youtube nocookie.com/embed/PuRUk4mV2jc\" title=\"pnpm Catalogs — A New Tool to Manage Dependencies in monorepos\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard write; encrypted media; gyroscope; picture in picture; fullscreen\" </iframe The Catalog Protocol ( ) Once a catalog is defined in , The protocol can be used instead of the version range itself. This is equivalent to wr The Catalog Protocol (`catalog:`) Advantages Defining Catalogs Default Catalog Named Catalogs Publishing Settings"},{"u":"cli/access.html","t":"pnpm access","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.11.0 Manages package access and visibility on the registry. Subcommands list packages List the packages a user, scope, or team can access. With no argument, your own packages are listed. The argument type is inferred from its shape: a value containing is a team, a value starting with is an organization, and anything else is a user. is accepted as an alias, so is equivalent to . list collaborators List the collaborators on a package, optionally filtered to a single user. get status Show whether a package is public or restricted. set status Set the package visibility. Only scoped p Subcommands list packages list collaborators get status set status set mfa grant revoke Options --registry &lt;url\\&gt; --json --otp &lt;code\\&gt;","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/add.html","t":"pnpm add <pkg>","n":"pnpm","b":"Installs a package and any packages that it depends on. By default, any new package is installed as a production dependency. TL;DR Command Meaning Save to Save to Save to Install package globally Install from the tag Specify version Supported package sources pnpm supports installing packages from various sources. See the Supported package sources page for detailed documentation on: npm registry JSR registry Workspace packages Local file system (tarballs and directories) Remote tarballs Git repositories (with semver, subdirectories, and more) Options save prod, P, p Install the specified packag TL;DR Supported package sources Options --save-prod, -P, -p --save-dev, -D, -d --save-optional, -O, -o --save-exact, -E, -e --save-peer --save-catalog --save-catalog-name &lt;catalog_name\\> --config --ignore-workspace-root-check --global, -g --workspace --allow-build --filter &lt;package_selector\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/approve-builds.html","t":"pnpm approve-builds","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v10.1.0 Approve dependencies for running scripts during installation. The approved dependencies are added to the [ ] map in with a value of , while unapproved ones are saved with a value of . You can also update these settings manually if you prefer. [ ]: ../settings/build.md allowbuilds Usage You can run without arguments to get an interactive prompt, or pass package names as positional arguments: Prefix a package name with to deny it. Only mentioned packages are affected; the rest are left untouched. During install, packages with ignored builds that are not yet listed in are automa Usage Options --all ~~--global, -g~~","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/audit.html","t":"pnpm audit","n":"pnpm","b":"Checks for known security issues with the installed packages. If security issues are found, try to update your dependencies via . If a simple update does not fix all the issues, use [overrides] to force versions that are not vulnerable. For instance, if is vulnerable, use this overrides to force : Or alternatively, run . If you want to tolerate some vulnerabilities as they don't affect your project, you may use the [ ] setting. Since v11, queries the registry's endpoint. The response does not include CVE identifiers, so advisories are filtered by GitHub advisory ID (GHSA) instead. If you previ Commands signatures Options --audit-level &lt;severity\\> --fix --interactive, -i --json --dev, -D --prod, -P --no-optional --ignore-registry-errors --ignore-unfixable --ignore &lt;vulnerability\\> Configuration audit.level audit.ignore","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/bin.html","t":"pnpm bin","n":"pnpm","b":"Prints the directory into which the executables of dependencies are linked. Options global, g Prints the location of the globally installed executables. Options --global, -g","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/bugs.html","t":"pnpm bugs","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.1.0 Opens a package's bug tracker URL in the browser. Aliases (added in v11.10.0) is an alias of . When run without arguments inside a package directory, it opens the bug tracker for the current project (using the field from ). When one or more package names are passed, pnpm fetches each package's metadata from the registry and opens its bug tracker. If a package does not declare a field, pnpm falls back to derived from the field. Examples Open the bug tracker for a published package: Open the bug tracker for multiple packages at once: Open the bug tracker for the current project Aliases Examples","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/cache-delete.html","t":"pnpm cache delete","n":"pnpm","b":":::warning This command is experimental ::: Deletes metadata cache for the specified package(s). Supports patterns. ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/cache-list-registries.html","t":"pnpm cache list-registries","n":"pnpm","b":":::warning This command is experimental ::: Lists all registries that have their metadata cache locally. ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/cache-list.html","t":"pnpm cache list","n":"pnpm","b":":::warning This command is experimental ::: Lists the available packages metadata cache. Supports filtering by glob. ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/cache-view.html","t":"pnpm cache view","n":"pnpm","b":":::warning This command is experimental ::: Views information from the specified package's cache. ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/cache.html","t":"pnpm cache","n":"pnpm","b":"Commands: cache list cache list registries cache delete cache view ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/cat-file.html","t":"pnpm cat-file","n":"pnpm","b":"Prints the contents of a file based on the hash value stored in the index file. For example: ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/cat-index.html","t":"pnpm cat-index","n":"pnpm","b":"Prints the index file of a specific package from the store. The package is specified by its name and version: ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/change.html","t":"pnpm change","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.13.0 Records a change intent: which packages a change affects, the bump type for each, and a summary that becomes the changelog entry. The intent file is written to in the changesets format. Change intents are consumed later by . See Release management for the whole workflow. Usage Run without arguments to record an intent interactively: You are asked three questions: 1. Which packages does this change affect? Packages changed since the merge base with (or ) are preselected. 2. Which packages should have a major bump? , then the same for . Anything left over is bumped as . 3. Sum Usage Subcommands status Options --bump &lt;type\\&gt; --summary &lt;text\\&gt; Referencing packages by directory","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/ci.html","t":"pnpm ci","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 Aliases: , , Perform a clean install. This command runs followed by . Designed for CI/CD environments where reproducible builds are critical. ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/clean.html","t":"pnpm clean","n":"pnpm","b":"Aliases: Safely remove contents from all workspace projects. Uses Node.js to remove directories, which correctly handles NTFS junctions on Windows without following them into their targets. In a workspace, directories are cleaned in the root and every workspace package. Non pnpm hidden entries (e.g., ) inside are preserved. If a custom is configured and it resides inside the project root (but outside ), it is also removed. Options lockfile, l Also remove files. Options --lockfile, -l","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/config.html","t":"pnpm config","n":"pnpm","b":"Aliases: Manage the configuration files. pnpm settings are split across two kinds of configuration files: Registry and authentication settings live in INI files — the global file and local files. All other pnpm settings live in YAML files — the global and the per project . The local workspace configuration file is located at the root of the project and is named . The global YAML configuration file ( ) is located at: If the $XDG CONFIG HOME env variable is set, then $XDG CONFIG HOME/pnpm/config.yaml On Windows: ~/AppData/Local/pnpm/config/config.yaml On macOS: ~/Library/Preferences/pnpm/config. Commands set &lt;key> &lt;value> get &lt;key> delete &lt;key> list Options --global, -g --location --json","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/create.html","t":"pnpm create","n":"pnpm","b":"Create a project from a or starter kit. Examples Options allow build Added in: v10.2.0 A list of package names that are allowed to run postinstall scripts during installation. Security and trust policies Since v11.0.0, honors the project level security and trust policy settings — (and its / companions) and (and its / companions) — when resolving and fetching the starter kit. Examples Options --allow-build Security and trust policies","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/dedupe.html","t":"pnpm dedupe","n":"pnpm","b":"Perform an install removing older dependencies in the lockfile if a newer version can be used. Options Check if running dedupe would result in changes without installing packages or editing the lockfile. Exits with a non zero status code if changes are possible. Options `--check`","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/deploy.html","t":"pnpm deploy","n":"pnpm","b":"Deploy a package from a workspace. During deployment, the files of the deployed package are copied to the target directory. All dependencies of the deployed package, including dependencies from the workspace, are installed inside an isolated directory at the target directory. The target directory will contain a portable package that can be copied to a server and executed without additional steps. :::note By default, the deploy command only works with workspaces that have the setting set to . If you want to use deploy without \"injected dependencies\", use the flag or set to . ::: :::note When th Options --dev, -D --no-optional --prod, -P --filter &lt;package_selector\\> --legacy Files included in the deployed project Configuration forceLegacyDeploy","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/deprecate.html","t":"pnpm deprecate","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 Set a deprecation message on a published package version. Consumers running will see this message when the matching version is installed. To clear a deprecation message, use or pass an empty string: Examples Deprecate a single version: Deprecate a range of versions: Deprecate all versions: Options registry &lt;url\\ The registry to publish to. Defaults to the registry configured for the package. otp &lt;code\\ When the registry requires two factor authentication, supply the one time password via this flag or the environment variable. pnpm undeprecate Remove a deprecation messag Examples Options --registry &lt;url\\> --otp &lt;code\\> pnpm undeprecate","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/dist-tag.html","t":"pnpm dist-tag","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 Manage distribution tags for a package. Dist tags provide human readable aliases (like , , ) that point to specific package versions on the registry. Subcommands add &lt;pkg\\ @&lt;version\\ [&lt;tag\\ ] Tag the specified version of a package with a dist tag. If is omitted, the value of the setting is used (defaults to ). rm &lt;pkg\\ &lt;tag\\ Remove a dist tag from a package. ls [&lt;pkg\\ ] List all dist tags for a package. If no package name is given, the dist tags for the current package (read from the local ) are shown. Options registry &lt;url\\ The registry to operate on. De Subcommands add &lt;pkg\\>@&lt;version\\> [&lt;tag\\>] rm &lt;pkg\\> &lt;tag\\> ls [&lt;pkg\\>] Options --registry &lt;url\\> --otp &lt;code\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/docs.html","t":"pnpm docs","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 Aliases: Open a package's documentation (or homepage) in the browser. When run without arguments inside a package directory, it opens the documentation for the current project. If the package does not declare a valid , pnpm falls back to . Examples Open the documentation for a published package: Open the documentation for multiple packages at once: Open the documentation for the current project: Examples","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/doctor.html","t":"pnpm doctor","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.14.0 Runs diagnostics on the pnpm installation and the environment it runs in. Each check reports how to fix what it finds, and the command exits with a non zero code when any check fails. Warnings do not fail the command. Checks Versions Reports the running pnpm and Node.js versions. Install method Reports how pnpm was installed — as the package or the standalone build — and warns when pnpm is being run by Corepack, which manages the pnpm version itself and makes unavailable. Global bin directory Checks that the directory pnpm links global executables into is on and writable. If Checks Versions Install method Global bin directory Cache directory Store directory Filesystem Registry connectivity Install smoke test Options --offline --benchmark --json","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/env.html","t":"pnpm env <cmd>","n":"pnpm","b":":::warning Deprecated is deprecated. Use instead. For example, becomes . ::: Manages the Node.js environment. :::danger does not include the binaries for Corepack. If you want to use Corepack to install other package managers, you need to install it separately (e.g. ). ::: <iframe width=\"560\" height=\"315\" src=\"https://www.youtube nocookie.com/embed/84 MzN 0Cng\" title=\"The pnpm patch command demo\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard write; encrypted media; gyroscope; picture in picture; fullscreen\" </iframe Commands use Install and use the specified version of Node.js Inst Commands use add remove, rm list, ls Options --global, -g","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/exec.html","t":"pnpm exec","n":"pnpm","b":"Execute a shell command in scope of a project. is added to the , so allows executing commands of dependencies. Examples If you have Jest as a dependency of your project, there is no need to install Jest globally, just run it with : The part is actually optional when the command is not in conflict with a builtin pnpm command, so you may also just run: Options Any options for the command should be listed before the keyword. Options listed after the keyword are passed to the executed command. Good. pnpm will run recursively: Bad, pnpm will not run recursively but will be executed with the option: Examples Options --recursive, -r Examples --no-reporter-hide-prefix --resume-from &lt;package_name\\> --parallel --shell-mode, -c --report-summary --filter &lt;package_selector\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/fetch.html","t":"pnpm fetch","n":"pnpm","b":"Fetch packages from a lockfile into virtual store, package manifest is ignored. Usage scenario This command is specifically designed to improve building a docker image. You may have read the [official guide] to writing a Dockerfile for a Node.js app, if you haven't read it yet, you may want to read it first. From that guide, we learn to write an optimized Dockerfile for projects using pnpm, which looks like As long as there are no changes to , , , , , docker build cache is still valid up to the layer of , which cost most of the time when building a docker image. However, modification to may ha Usage scenario Options --dev, -D --prod, -P","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/find-hash.html","t":"pnpm find-hash","n":"pnpm","b":":::warning This command is experimental ::: Lists the packages that include the file with the specified hash. For example: ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/help.html","t":"pnpm help <command>","n":"pnpm","b":"Display help information about pnpm. Options all, a Added in: v10.20.0 Print all the available commands. Options --all, -a","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/ignored-builds.html","t":"pnpm ignored-builds","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v10.1.0 Print the list of packages with blocked build scripts. ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/import.html","t":"pnpm import","n":"pnpm","b":"generates a from another package manager's lockfile. Supported source files: Note that if you have workspaces you wish to import dependencies for, they will need to be declared in a pnpm workspace.yaml file beforehand. ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/init.html","t":"pnpm init","n":"pnpm","b":"Create a file. Options bare Added in: v10.25.0 Creates a with only the required fields. init type &lt;type\\ Default: module Type: commonjs , module Set the module system for the package. init package manager Pin the project to the current pnpm version. Since v11, the pin is written as a entry (instead of the legacy field), so version ranges are supported and the resolved version is captured in . Inside a workspace subpackage this flag has no effect — the field is only added to the workspace root's . Options --bare --init-type &lt;type\\> --init-package-manager","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/install-test.html","t":"pnpm install-test","n":"pnpm","b":"Aliases: Runs followed immediately by . It takes exactly the same arguments as . ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/install.html","t":"pnpm install","n":"pnpm","b":"Aliases: is used to install all dependencies for a project. In a CI environment, installation fails if a lockfile is present but needs an update. Inside a [workspace], installs all dependencies in all the projects. If you want to disable this behavior, set the setting to . ! [workspace]: ../workspaces.md TL;DR Command Meaning Install offline from the store only is not updated Only is updated Preview changes without writing Options for filtering dependencies Without a lockfile, pnpm has to create one, and it must be consistent regardless of dependencies filtering, so running on a directory with TL;DR Options for filtering dependencies --prod, -P --dev, -D --no-optional --no-runtime Options --force --offline --prefer-offline --no-lockfile --lockfile-only --dry-run --fix-lockfile --update-checksums --frozen-lockfile --merge-git-branch-lockfiles --reporter=&lt;name\\> --shamefully-hoist --ignore-scripts --filter &lt;package_selector> --resolution-only","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/lane.html","t":"pnpm lane","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.13.0 Manages per package release lanes. A lane is a parallel release track: while a package is on one, the bare releases it as prereleases while the rest of the workspace keeps releasing stable versions. Moving a package back to the main lane releases its accumulated stable version on the next run. Membership lives under the key of ; this command is a convenience editor for that key. Usage Show lane membership If no package has been assigned a lane, this prints Move packages onto a lane is required — it selects the packages to move. Lane names may contain only alphanumerics and h Usage Show lane membership Move packages onto a lane Move packages back to the main lane Versions on a lane Options --filter &lt;package_selector\\&gt;","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/licenses.html","t":"pnpm licenses","n":"pnpm","b":"Commands list Aliases: List licenses for installed packages. Options dev, D Check only \"devDependencies\". json Show information in JSON format. long Show more details (such as a link to the repo) are not displayed. To display the details, pass this option. no optional Don't check packages from . prod, P Check only and . filter &lt;package selector\\ Read more about filtering. Commands list Options --dev, -D --json --long --no-optional --prod, -P --filter &lt;package_selector\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/link.html","t":"pnpm link","n":"pnpm","b":"Aliases: Links a local package to the current project's . Options Links package from directory to of package from where you're executing this command. must be a relative or absolute path. For example, if you are inside and you execute , then a link to will be created in . :::note Breaking changes in v11 no longer resolves packages from the global store. Only relative or absolute paths are accepted (use instead of ). has been removed. To register a local package's bins globally, use instead. with no arguments has been removed. Always pass an explicit path. ::: Use Cases Replace an installed pac Options `pnpm link <dir>` Use Cases Replace an installed package with a local version of it Add a binary globally What's the difference between `pnpm link` and using the `file:` protocol?","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/list.html","t":"pnpm list","n":"pnpm","b":"Aliases: This command will output all the versions of packages that are installed, as well as their dependencies, in a tree structure. Positional arguments are identifiers, which will limit the results to only the packages named. For example, . Options recursive, r Perform command on every package in subdirectories or on every workspace package, when executed inside a workspace. json Log output in JSON format. long Show extended information. lockfile only Added in: v10.23.0 Read package information from the lockfile instead of checking the actual directory. This is useful for quickly inspectin Options --recursive, -r --json --long --lockfile-only --parseable --global, -g --depth &lt;number\\> --prod, -P --dev, -D --no-optional --only-projects --exclude-peers --filter &lt;package_selector\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/login.html","t":"pnpm login","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 Aliases: Authenticate with an npm registry. Supports web based login with QR code as well as classic username/password authentication. Since v11.19.0, web based login no longer requires an interactive terminal: without a TTY, prints the authentication URL (skipping the QR code and the prompt to open the URL in a browser) and polls the registry until the browser approval completes. Only the classic username/password login still fails with in a non interactive terminal. Auth tokens are written to . Options registry &lt;url\\ The registry to authenticate with. Defaults to the con Options --registry &lt;url\\> --scope &lt;scope\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/logout.html","t":"pnpm logout","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 Log out of an npm registry. Revokes the authentication token on the registry and removes it from the local auth config file. If a scope is provided, the registry associated with that scope is used. The token is removed from . Options registry &lt;url\\ The registry to log out from. Defaults to the configured default registry. scope &lt;scope\\ Use the registry associated with the given scope. Options --registry &lt;url\\> --scope &lt;scope\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/outdated.html","t":"pnpm outdated","n":"pnpm","b":"Checks for outdated packages. The check can be limited to a subset of the installed packages by providing arguments (patterns are supported). Examples: Options recursive, r Check for outdated dependencies in every package found in subdirectories, or in every workspace package when executed inside a workspace. filter &lt;package selector\\ Read more about filtering. global, g List outdated global packages. long Print details. format &lt;format\\ Default: table Type: table , list , json Prints the outdated dependencies in the given format. compatible Prints only versions that satisfy specification Options --recursive, -r --filter &lt;package_selector\\> --global, -g --long --format &lt;format\\> --compatible --dev, -D --prod, -P --no-optional --sort-by --include-github-actions","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/owner.html","t":"pnpm owner","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.1.0 Aliases: Manages package owners on the registry. Commands ls Aliases: List all owners of a package. This is the default subcommand if no other subcommand is given. add Add a user as an owner of a package. Requires authentication. rm Remove a user from the list of owners of a package. Requires authentication. Options registry &lt;url\\ The base URL of the npm registry to use for the operation. Per scope and named registries (configured via and ) are respected for the package being modified. otp &lt;otp\\ When the registry requires two factor authentication, this option supplies  Commands ls add rm Options --registry &lt;url\\> --otp &lt;otp\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/pack-app.html","t":"pnpm pack-app","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 :::warning Experimental is experimental. Its flags, configuration schema, and output layout may change in future releases. ::: Pack a CommonJS entry file into a standalone executable for one or more target platforms, using the Node.js Single Executable Applications API under the hood. Each target produces an executable under (default ). On Windows targets the output is suffixed with ; macOS outputs are ad hoc signed automatically (via on macOS hosts or on Linux hosts) because SEA injection invalidates the existing code signature. Requirements The host must run Node.js v25.5+  Requirements Supported targets Known limitations `darwin-x64` binaries crash on Intel Macs Examples Options --entry &lt;path\\> --target, -t &lt;triplet\\> --runtime &lt;spec\\> --output-dir, -o &lt;dir\\> --output-name &lt;name\\> Configuration","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/pack.html","t":"pnpm pack","n":"pnpm","b":"Create a tarball from a package. Options recursive, r Added in: v10.11.0 Pack all packages from the workspace. out &lt;path\\ Customizes the output path for the tarball. Use and to include the package name and version, e.g., or . By default, the tarball is saved in the current working directory with the name . pack destination &lt;dir\\ Directory in which will save tarballs. The default is the current working directory. pack gzip level &lt;level\\ Specifying custom compression level. json Log output in JSON format. filter &lt;package selector\\ Added in: v10.11.0 Read more about filtering. dry run Options --recursive, -r --out &lt;path\\> --pack-destination &lt;dir\\> --pack-gzip-level &lt;level\\> --json --filter &lt;package_selector\\> --dry-run --skip-manifest-obfuscation Life Cycle Scripts","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/patch-commit.html","t":"pnpm patch-commit <path>","n":"pnpm","b":"Generate a patch out of a directory and save it (inspired by a similar command in Yarn). This command will compare the changes from to the package it was supposed to patch, generate a patch file, save the a patch file to (which can be customized by the option), and add an entry to [ ]. Usage: is the path to a modified copy of the patch target package, it is usually a temporary directory generated by . Options patches dir &lt;patchesDir The generated patch file will be saved to this directory. By default, patches are saved to the directory in the root of the project. [ ]: patch.md patcheddepend Options ---patches-dir &lt;patchesDir>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/patch-remove.html","t":"pnpm patch-remove <pkg...>","n":"pnpm","b":"Remove existing patch files and settings in . ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/patch.html","t":"pnpm patch <pkg>","n":"pnpm","b":"Prepare a package for patching (inspired by a similar command in Yarn). This command will cause a package to be extracted in a temporary directory intended to be editable at will. Once you're done with your changes, run (with being the temporary directory you received) to generate a patchfile and register it into your top level manifest via the [ ] field. Usage: [ ]: patcheddependencies :::note If you want to change the dependencies of a package, don't use patching to modify the file of the package. For overriding dependencies, use [overrides] or a [package hook]. ::: [overrides]: ../settings/ Options --edit-dir &lt;dir> --ignore-existing Configuration patchedDependencies allowUnusedPatches","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/peers.html","t":"pnpm peers","n":"pnpm","b":"Commands check Added in: v11.0.0 Checks for unmet and missing peer dependency issues by reading the lockfile. This command analyzes the lockfile and reports any packages with unresolved or incompatible peer dependencies. Commands check","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/ping.html","t":"pnpm ping","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 Ping the configured registry to verify connectivity. On success, the registry's response is printed. This is useful for quickly confirming that the current machine can reach the registry without installing or publishing anything. Options registry &lt;url\\ The registry to ping. Defaults to the configured default registry. Options --registry &lt;url\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/pkg.html","t":"pnpm pkg","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.3.0 Manages the contents of from the command line. Nested fields are addressed with dot separated paths (e.g. , ). Commands get Retrieves a value from . With no arguments, prints the full manifest. With one or more keys, prints the requested fields. When only one key is requested and it resolves to a string, the raw value is printed; otherwise the value is JSON encoded. Pass to always print JSON. set Sets one or more values in . Each argument has the form. By default the value is stored as a string. Pass to parse the value as JSON before storing it (useful for booleans, numbers,  Commands get set delete fix Options --json --recursive, -r","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/pm.html","t":"pnpm pm","n":"pnpm","b":"The syntax always runs the built in pnpm command, bypassing any same named script in . Some built in commands can be overridden by scripts. For example, if your project defines a script in , then runs that script instead of the built in . Using forces the built in command to run. Example Example","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/pnx.html","t":"pnx","n":"pnpm","b":"Aliases: , Fetches a package from the registry without installing it as a dependency, hotloads it, and runs whatever default command binary it exposes. For example, to use anywhere to bootstrap a Vue project without needing to install it under another project, you can run: This will fetch from the registry and run it with the given arguments. You may also specify which exact version of the package you'd like to use: The protocol is also supported, allowing you to use versions defined in your workspace catalogs: Options package &lt;name\\ The package to install before running the command. Exampl Options --package &lt;name\\> --allow-build --shell-mode, -c --silent, -s Security and trust policies","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/prefix.html","t":"pnpm prefix","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.10.0 Prints the current package prefix directory — the directory containing the closest . Options global, g Prints the global prefix directory instead. Options --global, -g","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/prune.html","t":"pnpm prune","n":"pnpm","b":"Removes unnecessary packages. Options prod Remove the packages specified in . no optional Remove the packages specified in . :::warning The prune command does not support recursive execution on a monorepo currently. To only install production dependencies in a monorepo folders can be deleted and then re installed with . ::: Options --prod --no-optional","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/publish.html","t":"pnpm publish","n":"pnpm","b":"Publishes a package to the registry. :::note Since v11, is implemented natively and no longer delegates to the CLI. If you rely on a feature that is now gone, please open an issue at pnpm/pnpm. As a workaround, you can still run . ::: When publishing a package inside a workspace, the LICENSE file from the root of the workspace is packed with the package (unless the package has a license of its own). You may override some fields before publish, using the [publishConfig] field in . You also can use the to customize the published subdirectory (usually using third party build tools). When running  Options --recursive, -r --json --tag &lt;tag\\> --access &lt;public|restricted\\> --no-git-checks --publish-branch &lt;branch\\> --force --batch --skip-manifest-obfuscation --report-summary --dry-run --otp --provenance --filter &lt;package_selector\\> Configuration Life Cycle Scripts","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/rebuild.html","t":"pnpm rebuild","n":"pnpm","b":"Aliases: Rebuild a package. Options recursive, r This command runs the pnpm rebuild command in every package of the monorepo. filter &lt;package selector\\ Read more about filtering. Options --recursive, -r --filter &lt;package_selector\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/recursive.html","t":"pnpm -r, --recursive","n":"pnpm","b":"Aliases: , , , Runs a command in every project of a workspace, when used with the following commands: Runs a command in every project of a workspace, excluding the root project, when used with the following commands: If you want the root project be included even when running scripts, set the [includeWorkspaceRoot] setting to . Usage example: Options link workspace packages Default: false Type: true, false, deep Link locally available packages in workspaces of a monorepo into instead of re downloading them from the registry. This emulates functionality similar to . When this is set to deep, loc Options --link-workspace-packages --workspace-concurrency --[no-]bail --[no-]sort --reverse --filter &lt;package_selector\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/remove.html","t":"pnpm remove","n":"pnpm","b":"Aliases: , , Removes packages from and from the project's . Options recursive, r When used inside a workspace, removes a dependency (or dependencies) from every workspace package. When used not inside a workspace, removes a dependency (or dependencies) from every package found in subdirectories. global, g Remove a global package. save dev, D Only remove the dependency from . save optional, O Only remove the dependency from . save prod, P Only remove the dependency from . filter &lt;package selector\\ Read more about filtering. Options --recursive, -r --global, -g --save-dev, -D --save-optional, -O --save-prod, -P --filter &lt;package_selector\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/repo.html","t":"pnpm repo","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.3.0 Opens the URL of a package's repository in the browser. With no arguments, opens the repository of the current project (read from the field of ). With one or more package names, fetches each package's metadata from the registry and opens its repository URL. The repository URL is normalized to its web equivalent — e.g. opens as . When the field includes a , the URL points at that subdirectory inside the repo. Examples Options registry &lt;url\\ The registry from which to fetch package metadata when an explicit package name is given. Per scope and named registries (configured vi Examples Options --registry &lt;url\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/root.html","t":"pnpm root","n":"pnpm","b":"Prints the effective modules directory. Options global, g The global package's modules directory is printed. Options --global, -g","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/run.html","t":"pnpm run","n":"pnpm","b":"Aliases: Runs a script defined in the package's manifest file. Examples Let's say you have a script configured in your , like so: You can now run that script by using ! Simple, right? Another thing to note for those that like to save keystrokes and time is that all scripts get aliased in as pnpm commands, so ultimately is just shorthand for (ONLY for scripts that do not share the same name as already existing pnpm commands). Running multiple scripts You may run multiple scripts at the same time by using a regex instead of the script name. Run all scripts that start with : The selector must be  Examples Running multiple scripts Details Environment Options --recursive, -r --if-present --no-bail --parallel --sequential, -s --stream --aggregate-output --resume-from &lt;package_name\\> --report-summary --reporter-hide-prefix --filter &lt;package_selector\\> pnpm-workspace.yaml settings","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/runtime.html","t":"pnpm runtime <cmd>","n":"pnpm","b":"Manage runtimes. Alias: Commands set Install the specified version of a runtime. Supported runtimes Node.js Deno Bun :::info Since v11.0.0, installing a Node.js runtime (via or ) does not extract the bundled , , and from the Node.js archive. This roughly halves the number of files pnpm has to hash, write to the CAS, and link during a runtime install. If you still need , install it separately with . ::: Examples Install Node.js v22 globally: Install the LTS version of Node.js: Install the latest version of Node.js: Install a prerelease version of Node.js: Install an LTS version of Node.js using Commands set Supported runtimes Examples Options --global, -g","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/sbom.html","t":"pnpm sbom","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 Generate a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) for the project. Supported formats: CycloneDX 1.7 (JSON) SPDX 2.3 (JSON) Usage Inside a workspace, supports filtering. When a single workspace package is selected, the root component in the SBOM uses that package's metadata. CycloneDX output marks components reachable only through with and the property. Runtime components, including installed optional dependencies, use the default required scope. Options sbom format &lt;cyclonedx spdx\\ The SBOM output format. This option is required. Supported values: , . sbom type &lt;library appl Usage Options --sbom-format &lt;cyclonedx|spdx\\> --sbom-type &lt;library|application\\> --sbom-spec-version &lt;version\\> --lockfile-only --sbom-authors &lt;names\\> --sbom-supplier &lt;name\\> --out &lt;path\\> --split --exclude-peers --prod, -P --dev, -D --no-optional --filter &lt;package_selector\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/search.html","t":"pnpm search","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 Aliases: , , Search the registry for packages matching the given keywords. Examples Options json Output search results in JSON format. search limit &lt;number\\ Default: 20 Maximum number of results to show. Examples Options --json --search-limit &lt;number\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/self-update.html","t":"pnpm self-update","n":"pnpm","b":"Updates pnpm to the latest version or the one specified. Usage examples: Behavior The behavior of depends on the project context: In a project that pins pnpm When the project's has a field set to pnpm (or a entry for pnpm), only updates the pinned version in to the resolved one. It does not install pnpm globally. The next time you run a pnpm command, pnpm will automatically download and switch to the specified version. Outside a project (or when the pnpm pin is ignored) If the project does not pin pnpm, or the pin is being ignored via , installs the resolved pnpm version globally and links it  Behavior In a project that pins pnpm Outside a project (or when the pnpm pin is ignored) Project settings are ignored Installing pnpm v12 (the Rust port)","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/set-script.html","t":"pnpm set-script","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.3.0 Aliases: Adds or updates an entry in the field of the project manifest. Supports , , and manifest formats. If the field does not exist, it is created. If a script with the same name already exists, it is overwritten. Examples The above is equivalent to manually editing : See also — set arbitrary fields in , including individual scripts via . Examples See also","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/setup.html","t":"pnpm setup","n":"pnpm","b":"This command is used by the standalone installation scripts of pnpm. For instance, in [https://get.pnpm.io/install.sh]. Setup does the following actions: creates a home directory for the pnpm CLI adds the pnpm home directory to the by updating the shell configuration file copies the pnpm executable to the pnpm home directory Since v11.18.0, when running on GitHub Actions, also appends and the global bin directory to the GitHub Actions environment files ( and ), so later steps in the same job can run and other global commands. :::tip After upgrading to pnpm v11, run to update your shell configu ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/stage.html","t":"pnpm stage","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.3.0 Stages packages for publishing using npm's staged publishing workflow. Staged versions are not resolved by until they are explicitly approved, letting you defer proof of presence (2FA) to a later point in time — useful for verifying release artifacts, smoke testing CI, or coordinating multi package releases. Subcommands publish Stage a package for publishing. Accepts the same arguments as , but uploads the tarball to staging instead of promoting it to the live registry. The resulting stage id is printed and can be used with the other subcommands. Use (or ) to stage every publ Subcommands publish list view approve reject download Options --registry &lt;url\\> --tag &lt;tag\\> --access &lt;public|restricted\\> --json --dry-run --otp &lt;otp\\> --recursive, -r --filter &lt;package_selector\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/star.html","t":"pnpm star","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 Mark a package as a favorite on the registry. You must be logged in (see ). pnpm unstar Remove a package from your favorites. pnpm stars List the packages you (or another user) have starred. When run without a username, pnpm lists the packages starred by the currently authenticated user. pnpm unstar pnpm stars","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/start.html","t":"pnpm start","n":"pnpm","b":"Aliases: Runs an arbitrary command specified in the package's property of its object. If no property is specified on the object, it will attempt to run as a default, failing if neither are present. The intended usage of the property is to specify a command that starts your program. ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/store.html","t":"pnpm store","n":"pnpm","b":"Managing the package store. Commands status Checks for modified packages in the store. Returns exit code 0 if the content of the package is the same as it was at the time of unpacking. add Functionally equivalent to [ ], except this adds new packages to the store directly without modifying any projects or files outside of the store. [ ]: add.md prune Removes unreferenced packages from the store. Unreferenced packages are packages that are not used by any projects on the system. Packages can become unreferenced after most installation operations, for instance when dependencies are made redundan Commands status add prune path","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/team.html","t":"pnpm team","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.13.0 Manages organization teams and team memberships on the registry. Team references are always written with a leading : for an organization and for a team within it. Subcommands create Create a new team in an organization. destroy Destroy an existing team. add Add a user to an existing team. rm Remove a user from an existing team. ls List the teams in an organization, or the members of a team. Aliases: . If no subcommand is given and the first argument looks like a scope or team, is assumed, so is equivalent to . Options registry &lt;url\\&gt; The base URL of the npm registry to Subcommands create destroy add rm ls Options --registry &lt;url\\&gt; --otp &lt;code\\&gt; --parseable --json","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/test.html","t":"pnpm test","n":"pnpm","b":"Aliases: , , Runs an arbitrary command specified in the package's property of its object. The intended usage of the property is to specify a command that runs unit or integration testing for your program. ","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/unlink.html","t":"pnpm unlink","n":"pnpm","b":"Unlinks a system wide package (inverse of ). If called without arguments, all linked dependencies will be unlinked inside the current project. This is similar to , except pnpm re installs the dependency after removing the external link. :::info If you want to remove a link made with , you should use . only removes the links in your current directory. ::: Options recursive, r Unlink in every package found in subdirectories or in every workspace package, when executed inside a workspace. filter &lt;package selector\\ Read more about filtering. Options --recursive, -r --filter &lt;package_selector\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/unpublish.html","t":"pnpm unpublish","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 Remove a published package version from the registry. :::warning Unpublishing is generally discouraged. Most registries (including the public npm registry) restrict when and how packages can be unpublished. Prefer whenever possible. ::: Examples Unpublish a specific version: Unpublish a range of versions using a semver specifier: Unpublish an entire package (all versions). Requires : When run without arguments inside a package directory, pnpm unpublishes the current package version read from the local . Options force Required when removing an entire package (all versions) rat Examples Options --force --registry &lt;url\\> --otp &lt;code\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/update.html","t":"pnpm update","n":"pnpm","b":"Aliases: , updates packages to their latest version based on the specified range. When used without arguments, updates all dependencies. TL;DR Command Meaning Updates all dependencies, adhering to ranges specified in Updates all dependencies to their latest versions Updates to the latest version on v2 Updates all dependencies under the scope Selecting dependencies with patterns You can use patterns to update specific dependencies. Update all packages: Update all dependencies, except : Patterns may also be combined, so the next command will update all packages, except : Updating GitHub Actions  TL;DR Selecting dependencies with patterns Updating GitHub Actions Options --recursive, -r --latest, -L --global, -g --workspace --prod, -P --dev, -D --no-optional --interactive, -i --no-save --changeset --include-github-actions --filter &lt;package_selector\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/version.html","t":"pnpm version","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 Bump the package version. can be any of the bump types above or an explicit semver version (e.g. ). Workspaces and the protocol are supported, so cross references between workspace packages are updated correctly. When run inside a git repository, creates a git commit and an annotated tag for the bump. The working tree must be clean (see below) and commits/tags can be disabled with . Git commits and tags are always skipped in recursive mode because multiple packages may be bumped to different versions in a single run. Usage Recursive releases Added in: v11.13.0 Run with and no Usage Recursive releases Options --preid &lt;prerelease-id\\> --message, -m &lt;message\\> --tag-version-prefix &lt;prefix\\> --no-git-tag-version --no-commit-hooks --sign-git-tag --no-git-checks --allow-same-version --recursive, -r --json","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/view.html","t":"pnpm view","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 Aliases: , View package metadata from the registry. Usage Show all metadata for a package: Show a specific field: Show metadata for a specific version: When no package name is provided, searches upward for the nearest project manifest ( , , or ) and uses its field. If the manifest exists but has no , the command fails. Options json Output the metadata in JSON format. Usage Options --json","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/whoami.html","t":"pnpm whoami","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 Print the username associated with the current registry credentials. If you are not logged in, the command exits with an error. Use to authenticate first. Options registry &lt;url\\ The registry to check. Defaults to the configured default registry. Options --registry &lt;url\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/why.html","t":"pnpm why","n":"pnpm","b":"Shows all packages that depend on the specified package. The output is a reverse dependency tree: the searched package appears at the root, with its dependents as branches, walking back to workspace roots. Duplicate subtrees are deduplicated in the output and shown as \"deduped\". Options recursive, r Show the dependency tree for the specified package on every package in subdirectories or on every workspace package when executed inside a workspace. json Show information in JSON format. long Show verbose output. parseable Show parseable output instead of tree view. global, g List packages in the  Options --recursive, -r --json --long --parseable --global, -g --prod, -P --dev, -D --depth &lt;number\\> --only-projects --exclude-peers --filter &lt;package_selector\\>","s":"cli"},{"u":"cli/with.html","t":"pnpm with","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.0.0 Run pnpm at a specific version (or the currently running one) for a single invocation, ignoring the and fields of the project's manifest. The downloaded pnpm is installed using the same mechanism as and cached in the global virtual store for reuse on subsequent runs. Examples Run the globally installed pnpm, ignoring the version pinned in the manifest: Run a specific version: Use a dist tag: Related settings pmOnFail If you want to permanently skip the / check (for example, because version management is handled by asdf, mise, Volta, or a similar tool), set the setting to inst Examples Related settings pmOnFail","s":"cli"},{"u":"completion.html","t":"Command line tab-completion","n":"pnpm","b":":::info Completion for pnpm v9+ is incompatible with completion for older pnpm versions. If you have already installed pnpm completion for a version older than v9, you must uninstall it first to ensure that completion for v9+ works properly. You can do this by removing the section of code that contains in your dot files. ::: Unlike other popular package managers, which usually require plugins, pnpm supports command line tab completion for Bash, Zsh, Fish, and similar shells. To setup autocompletion for Bash, run: To setup autocompletion for Fish, run: g plane/pnpm shell completion [pnpm shell  g-plane/pnpm-shell-completion"},{"u":"config-dependencies.html","t":"Config Dependencies","n":"pnpm","b":"Config dependencies allow you to share and centralize configuration files, settings, and hooks across multiple projects. They are installed before all regular dependencies (\"dependencies\", \"devDependencies\", \"optionalDependencies\"), making them ideal for setting up custom hooks, patches, and catalog entries. Config dependencies help you keep all the hooks, settings, patches, overrides, catalogs, rules in a single place and use them across multiple repositories. If your config dependency is named following the , , or pattern, pnpm will automatically load its (falling back to ) from the package  How to Add a Config Dependency Platform-specific binaries via `optionalDependencies` Usage Installing Dependencies Used in Hooks Updating pnpm Settings Dynamically Loading Patch Files"},{"u":"configuring.html","t":"Configuring","n":"pnpm","b":"pnpm settings are divided into two categories: Authentication and certificate settings are stored in INI files. These contain sensitive credentials and should not be committed to your repository. See Authentication Settings for details. All other settings are stored in YAML files: the project and the global . pnpm also no longer reads settings from the field of . Settings should be defined in . Local project configuration Project level settings go in : Global configuration The global YAML config file ( ) is located at one of the following paths: If the $XDG CONFIG HOME env variable is set, the Local project configuration Global configuration Environment variables"},{"u":"continuous-integration.html","t":"Continuous Integration","n":"pnpm","b":"pnpm can easily be used in various continuous integration systems. :::note In all the provided configuration files the store is cached. However, this is not required, and it is not guaranteed that caching the store will make installation faster. So feel free to not cache the pnpm store in your job. ::: :::important Only cache pnpm's store and cache directories in locations writable by trusted jobs. Do not let untrusted CI jobs write to a store or metadata cache that trusted jobs later restore. These directories are trusted caches; see the and settings for details. ::: :::tip Lockfile behavior  AppVeyor Azure Pipelines Bitbucket Pipelines CircleCI GitHub Actions GitLab CI Jenkins Semaphore Travis"},{"u":"docker.html","t":"Working with Docker","n":"pnpm","b":":::note It is impossible to create reflinks or hardlinks between a Docker container and the host filesystem during build time. The next best thing you can do is using BuildKit cache mount to share cache between builds. Alternatively, you may use [podman] because it can mount Btrfs volumes during build time. If you use BuildKit cache mounts, keep the pnpm store cache scoped to mutually trusted builds. A store cache that can be written by an untrusted build should not be reused by trusted builds. ::: [podman]: podman.md Official pnpm base image An official pnpm base image is published to GitHub  Official pnpm base image Tags Installing Node.js When to use this image Minimizing Docker image size and build time Example 1: Build a bundle in a Docker container Example 2: Build multiple Docker images in a monorepo Example 3: Build on CI/CD"},{"u":"errors.html","t":"Error Codes","n":"pnpm","b":"ERR PNPM UNEXPECTED STORE A modules directory is present and is linked to a different store directory. If you changed the store directory intentionally, run and pnpm will reinstall the dependencies using the new store. ERR PNPM NO MATCHING VERSION INSIDE WORKSPACE A project has a workspace dependency that does not exist in the workspace. For instance, package has in the : However, there is only in the workspace, so will fail. To fix this error, all dependencies that use the [workspace protocol] should be updated to use versions of packages that are present in the workspace. This can be done ei ERR_PNPM_UNEXPECTED_STORE ERR_PNPM_NO_MATCHING_VERSION_INSIDE_WORKSPACE ERR_PNPM_PEER_DEP_ISSUES ERR_PNPM_OUTDATED_LOCKFILE ERR\\_PNPM\\_TARBALL\\_INTEGRITY ERR_PNPM_MISMATCHED_RELEASE_CHANNEL ERR_PNPM_INVALID_NODE_VERSION"},{"u":"faq.html","t":"Frequently Asked Questions","n":"pnpm","b":"Why does my folder use disk space if packages are stored in a global store? pnpm creates [hard links] from the global store to the project's folders. Hard links point to the same place on the disk where the original files are. So, for example, if you have in your project as a dependency and it occupies 1MB of space, then it will look like it occupies 1MB of space in the project's folder and the same amount of space in the global store. However, that 1MB is the same space on the disk addressed from two different locations. So in total occupies 1MB, not 2MB. [hard links]: https://en.wikipedia.or Why does my `node_modules` folder use disk space if packages are stored in a global store? Does it work on Windows? But the nested `node_modules` approach is incompatible with Windows? What about circular symlinks? Why have hard links at all? Why not symlink directly to the global store? Does pnpm work across different subvolumes in one Btrfs partition? Does pnpm work across multiple drives or filesystems? Store path is specified Store path is NOT specified What does `pnpm` stand for? `pnpm` does not work with &lt;YOUR-PROJECT-HERE>? Solution 1 Solution 2 Solution 3"},{"u":"feature-comparison.html","t":"Feature Comparison","n":"pnpm","b":"Feature pnpm Yarn npm Notes : : : : : : [Workspace support] :white check mark: :white check mark: :white check mark: Isolated :white check mark: :white check mark: :white check mark: Default in pnpm. [Hoisted ] :white check mark: :white check mark: :white check mark: Default in npm. Plug'n'Play :white check mark: :white check mark: :x: Default in Yarn. [Autoinstalling peers] :white check mark: :x: :white check mark: Zero Installs :x: :white check mark: :x: [Patching dependencies] :white check mark: :white check mark: :x: [Managing runtimes] :white check mark: :x: :x: [Managing versions of itse "},{"u":"filtering.html","t":"Filtering","n":"pnpm","b":"Filtering allows you to restrict commands to specific subsets of packages. pnpm supports a rich selector syntax for picking packages by name or by relation. Selectors may be specified via the (or ) flag: Matching filter &lt;package name To select an exact package, just specify its name ( ) or use a pattern to select a set of packages ( ). Examples: Specifying the scope of the package is optional, so will pick if is not found. However, if the workspace has multiple packages with the same name (for instance, and ), then filtering without scope will pick nothing. filter &lt;package name ... To se Matching --filter &lt;package_name> --filter &lt;package_name>... --filter &lt;package_name>^... --filter ...&lt;package_name> --filter \"...^&lt;package_name>\" --filter `./<glob>`, --filter `{<glob>}` --filter \"[&lt;since>]\" --fail-if-no-match Excluding Multiplicity --filter-prod &lt;filtering_pattern> --test-pattern &lt;glob> --changed-files-ignore-pattern &lt;glob>"},{"u":"finders.html","t":"Finders","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v10.16.0 Finder functions let you search your dependency graph by any property of a package, not just its name. They can be declared in [.pnpmfile.mjs] and used with [pnpm list] and [pnpm why]. [.pnpmfile.mjs]: pnpmfile.md [pnpm list]: cli/list.md [pnpm why]: cli/why.md Defining finder functions Finder functions are declared in your project’s [.pnpmfile.mjs] file under the finders export. Each function receives a context object and must return either: → include this dependency in the results, → skip it, or a → include this dependency and print the string as additional info. Example:  Defining finder functions Finder context (ctx) Using finders Returning extra metadata"},{"u":"git-branch-lockfiles.html","t":"Git Branch Lockfiles","n":"pnpm","b":"Git branch lockfiles allows you to totally avoid lockfile merge conflicts and solve it later. Use git branch lockfiles You can turn on this feature by configuring the file. By doing this, lockfile name will be generated based on the current branch name. For instance, the current branch name is . Then, the generated lockfile name will be . You can commit it to the Git, and merge all git branch lockfiles later. :::note is special in that the is automatically converted to , so the corresponding lockfile name would be . ::: Merge git branch lockfiles To merge all git branch lockfiles, just specify Use git branch lockfiles Merge git branch lockfiles `pnpm install --merge-git-branch-lockfiles` Branch Matching"},{"u":"git-worktrees.html","t":"pnpm + Git Worktrees for Multi-Agent Development","n":"pnpm","b":"When multiple AI agents need to work on the same monorepo simultaneously, they each need an isolated working copy with fully functional . Git worktrees combined with pnpm's global virtual store make this practical: each worktree gets its own checkout and its own , but dependencies are shared across all of them through a single content addressable store on disk. What is a git worktree? Normally, a git repository has a single working directory tied to one branch at a time. If you want to look at another branch, you have to stash or commit your changes and switch. A git worktree lets you check ou What is a git worktree? Why worktrees? Why this matters even more with AI agents Setting it up 1. Create a bare repository 2. Create worktrees for each branch 3. Enable the global virtual store 4. Install dependencies in each worktree How it works Example: the pnpm monorepo itself Tips"},{"u":"git.html","t":"Working with Git","n":"pnpm","b":"Lockfiles You should always commit the lockfile ( ). This is for a multitude of reasons, the primary of which being: it enables faster installation for CI and production environments, due to being able to skip package resolution it enforces consistent installations and resolution between development, testing, and production environments, meaning the packages used in testing and production will be exactly the same as when you developed your project Merge conflicts pnpm can automatically resolve merge conflicts in . If you have conflicts, just run and commit the changes. Be warned, however. It i Lockfiles Merge conflicts"},{"u":"global-packages.html","t":"Global Packages","n":"pnpm","b":"Global packages are CLI tools and utilities installed system wide with . In pnpm v11, global package management was redesigned for better isolation and reliability. Installing global packages For example: Isolated installations Each globally installed package (or group of packages installed together) gets its own isolated installation directory with its own , , and lockfile. This prevents global packages from interfering with each other through peer dependency conflicts, hoisting changes, or version resolution shifts. Isolated installations are stored at , where the hash is derived from the se Installing global packages Isolated installations Directory layout Listing global packages Managing global packages Binaries location Global virtual store Registering local packages globally Build script approval"},{"u":"global-virtual-store.html","t":"Global Virtual Store","n":"pnpm","b":"By default, pnpm creates a directory inside each project's — this is the \"virtual store\". It contains hardlinks to files in the content addressable store. Every project gets its own projection of this virtual store — pnpm hardlinks files from the content addressable store into the directory structure. The actual file contents exist only once on disk, but the directory structure is recreated for each project so that Node.js's module resolution algorithm can find the right dependencies for each package. The global virtual store ( ) changes this. Instead of each project having its own directory,  Default behavior vs global virtual store Default (per-project virtual store) With global virtual store How package identity works When to use it Limitations Global packages Configuration"},{"u":"how-peers-are-resolved.html","t":"How peers are resolved","n":"pnpm","b":"One of the best features of pnpm is that in one project, a specific version of a package will always have one set of dependencies. There is one exception from this rule, though packages with [peer dependencies]. [peer dependencies]: https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/v10/configuring npm/package json peerdependencies Peer dependencies are resolved from dependencies installed higher in the dependency graph, since they share the same version as their parent. That means that if has two peers ( and ) then it might have multiple different sets of dependencies in the same project. In the example above, is in "},{"u":"installation.html","t":"Installation","n":"pnpm","b":"Prerequisites If you don't use the standalone script or to install pnpm, then you need to have Node.js (at least v22) to be installed on your system. :::info Looking for pnpm 12? It is currently in beta and installed differently from pnpm 11. See Installing the pnpm 12 beta. ::: Using a standalone script You may install pnpm even if you don't have Node.js installed, using the following scripts. On Windows :::warning Sometimes, Windows Defender may block our executable if you install pnpm this way. Due to this issue, we currently recommend installing pnpm using npm or Corepack on Windows. ::: U Prerequisites Using a standalone script On Windows On POSIX systems In a Docker container Installing a specific version Using Corepack Using other package managers Using npm Using Homebrew Using winget Using Scoop Using Choco Installing the pnpm 12 beta Using pnpm {#pnpm-12-using-pnpm} Using npm {#pnpm-12-using-npm} Using a standalone script {#pnpm-12-using-a-standalone-script} Compatibility Troubleshooting Updating pnpm Uninstalling pnpm"},{"u":"limitations.html","t":"Limitations","n":"pnpm","b":"1. and are ignored. Unlike pnpm, npm can install the same multiple times and with different sets of dependencies. npm's lockfile is designed to reflect the flat layout, however, as pnpm creates an isolated layout by default, it cannot respect npm's lockfile format. See [pnpm import] if you wish to convert a lockfile to pnpm's format, though. 1. Binstubs (files in ) are always shell files, not symlinks to JS files. The shell files are created to help pluggable CLI apps in finding their plugins in the unusual structure. This is very rarely an issue and if you expect the file to be a JS file, ref "},{"u":"logos.html","t":"Logos","n":"pnpm","b":"Standard logo SVG: ! PNG: ! Standard logo with no text SVG: ! PNG: ! Standard light logo SVG: ! PNG: ! Standard light logo with no text SVG: ! PNG: ! Standard logo Standard logo with no text Standard light logo Standard light logo with no text"},{"u":"migration.html","t":"Migrating from v10 to v11","n":"pnpm","b":"pnpm v11 introduces several breaking changes to how configuration is read and which settings are available. Most config changes are mechanical and can be applied by a codemod; the remainder require human attention. pnpm prints a pointer to this page when is run from a v10 install. Run the codemod The codemod applies the following automatically: Moves settings out of into . In v11, pnpm no longer reads configuration from the field in . Splits into auth/registry vs. everything else . v11 only reads auth and registry settings from . Every other setting ( , , , …) is moved into with a camelCase ke Run the codemod Manual follow-ups"},{"u":"motivation.html","t":"Motivation","n":"pnpm","b":"Saving disk space !An illustration of the pnpm content addressable store. On the illustration there are two projects with node modules. The files in the node modules directories are hard links to the same files in the content addressable store. When using npm, if you have 100 projects using a dependency, you will have 100 copies of that dependency saved on disk. With pnpm, the dependency will be stored in a content addressable store, so: 1. If you depend on different versions of the dependency, only the files that differ are added to the store. For instance, if it has 100 files, and a new vers Saving disk space Boosting installation speed Creating a non-flat node_modules directory"},{"u":"npmrc.html","t":"Authentication Settings","n":"pnpm","b":"The settings on this page contain sensitive credentials and are stored in INI formatted files. Do not commit these files to your repository. For non sensitive settings (proxy, SSL, registries, etc.), see Settings (pnpm workspace.yaml). Auth file locations pnpm reads authentication settings from the following files, in order of priority (highest first): 1. — project level auth. This file should be listed in . 2. — the primary user level auth file. writes tokens here. 3. — read as a fallback for easier migration from npm. Use the setting to point to a different file. The directory is: If the $XD Auth file locations Environment variables in auth settings Authentication Settings &lt;URL&gt;&#58;_authToken Scope-specific auth tokens &lt;URL&gt;&#58;tokenHelper _auth Certificate Settings ca cafile &lt;URL&gt;&#58;cafile &lt;URL&gt;&#58;ca cert &lt;URL&gt;&#58;cert &lt;URL&gt;&#58;certfile key &lt;URL&gt;&#58;key &lt;URL&gt;&#58;keyfile"},{"u":"only-allow-pnpm.html","t":"Only allow pnpm","n":"pnpm","b":"When you use pnpm on a project, you don't want others to accidentally run or . To prevent devs from using other package managers, you can add the following script to your : Now, whenever someone runs or , they'll get an error instead and installation will not proceed. If you use npm v7, use instead. "},{"u":"package-json.html","t":"package.json","n":"pnpm","b":"The manifest file of a package. It contains all the package's metadata, including dependencies, title, author, et cetera. This is a standard preserved across all major Node.js package managers, including pnpm. In addition to the traditional format, pnpm also supports (via [json5]) and (via [js yaml]). [json5]: https://www.npmjs.com/package/json5 [js yaml]: https://www.npmjs.com/package/@zkochan/js yaml :::note Since v11, pnpm no longer reads settings from the field of . Settings must be defined in instead. See Configuring. ::: engines You can specify the version of Node and pnpm that your soft engines engines.runtime devEngines.runtime devEngines.packageManager dependenciesMeta dependenciesMeta.*.injected peerDependencies peerDependenciesMeta peerDependenciesMeta.*.optional publishConfig publishConfig.name publishConfig.executableFiles publishConfig.directory publishConfig.linkDirectory"},{"u":"package-sources.html","t":"Supported package sources","n":"pnpm","b":"pnpm supports installing packages from various sources. These sources are divided into two categories: trusted sources and exotic sources . Exotic sources (like Git repositories or direct tarball URLs) can introduce supply chain risks when used by transitive dependencies. You can prevent transitive dependencies from using exotic sources by setting [ ] to . [ ]: settings/dependency resolution.md blockexoticsubdeps Trusted sources Trusted sources are considered safe for both direct and transitive dependencies. npm registry will install the latest version of from the npm registry by default. If e Trusted sources npm registry JSR registry Workspace Local file system Exotic sources Remote tarball Git repository Install from a Git repository using semver Install from a subdirectory of a Git repository Install from a Git repository via a full URL Install from a Git repository using hosting providers shorthand Install from a Git repository combining different parameters"},{"u":"pnpm-cli.html","t":"pnpm CLI","n":"pnpm","b":"Short aliases Added in: v11.0.0 is available as a short alias for , and as a short alias for . You can use them anywhere you'd use or : Differences vs npm Unlike npm, pnpm validates all options. For example, will fail as is not a valid option for . However, some dependencies may use the environment variable, which is populated from the CLI options. In this case, you have the following options: 1. explicitly set the env variable: 1. force the unknown option with : Options C &lt;path\\ , dir &lt;path\\ Run as if pnpm was started in instead of the current working directory. w, workspace root Run as Short aliases Differences vs npm Options -C &lt;path\\>, --dir &lt;path\\> -w, --workspace-root Commands Environment variables"},{"u":"pnpm-vs-npm.html","t":"pnpm vs npm","n":"pnpm","b":"npm's flat tree npm maintains a [flattened dependency tree] as of version 3. This leads to less disk space bloat, with a messy directory as a side effect. On the other hand, pnpm manages by using hard linking and symbolic linking to a global on disk content addressable store. This lets you get the benefits of far less disk space usage, while also keeping your clean. There is documentation on the [store layout] if you wish to learn more. The good thing about pnpm's proper structure is that it \"[helps to avoid silly bugs]\" by making it impossible to use modules that are not specified in the proj npm's flat tree Installation Directory dependencies"},{"u":"pnpmfile.html","t":".pnpmfile.mjs","n":"pnpm","b":"pnpm lets you hook directly into the installation process via special functions (hooks). Hooks can be declared in a file called (ESM) or (CommonJS). By default, should be located in the same directory as the lockfile. For instance, in a workspace with a shared lockfile, should be in the root of the monorepo. Hooks TL;DR Hook Function Process Uses Called after pnpm parses the dependency's package manifest Allows you to mutate a dependency's . Called after the dependencies have been resolved. Allows you to mutate the lockfile. Called before creating a tarball during pack/publish Allows you to cu Hooks TL;DR `hooks.readPackage(pkg, context): pkg | Promise<pkg>` Arguments Usage Known limitations `hooks.updateConfig(config): config | Promise<config>` Usage example `hooks.afterAllResolved(lockfile, context): lockfile | Promise<lockfile>` Arguments Usage example Known Limitations `hooks.beforePacking(pkg): pkg | Promise<pkg>` Arguments Usage example `hooks.preResolution(options): Promise<void>` Arguments `hooks.importPackage(destinationDir, options): Promise<string | undefined>` Arguments `hooks.fetchers` Finders Custom Resolvers and Fetchers TypeScript Interfaces Custom Resolvers Resolve"},{"u":"podman.html","t":"Working with Podman","n":"pnpm","b":"Sharing Files Between a Container and the Host Btrfs Filesystem :::note This method only works on copy on write filesystems supported by Podman, such as Btrfs. For other filesystems, like Ext4, pnpm will copy the files instead. ::: Podman support copy on write filesystems like Btrfs. With Btrfs, container runtimes create actual Btrfs subvolumes for their mounted volumes. pnpm can leverage this behavior to reflink the files between different mounted volumes. To share files between the host and the container, mount the store directory and the directory from the host to the container. This allows Sharing Files Between a Container and the Host Btrfs Filesystem"},{"u":"production.html","t":"Production","n":"pnpm","b":"There are two ways to bootstrap your package in a production environment with pnpm. One of these is to commit the lockfile. Then, in your production environment, run this will build the dependency tree using the lockfile, meaning the dependency versions will be consistent with how they were when the lockfile was committed. This is the most effective way (and the one we recommend) to ensure your dependency tree persists across environments. The other method is to commit the lockfile AND copy the package store to your production environment (you can change where with the [store location option]) "},{"u":"scripts.html","t":"Scripts","n":"pnpm","b":"How pnpm handles the field of . Hidden Scripts Added in: v11.0.0 Scripts with names starting with are hidden. They cannot be run directly via and are omitted from the listing. Hidden scripts can only be called from other scripts. In this example, would fail, but would succeed because is called from another script. Environment Variables pnpm sets the following environment variables during lifecycle script execution: — the package name — the package version — the name of the running script (e.g., ) :::note Since v11, pnpm no longer populates environment variables from the pnpm configuration. Onl Hidden Scripts Environment Variables Built-in Command and Script Name Conflicts Lifecycle Scripts `pnpm:devPreinstall`"},{"u":"settings/build.html","t":"Build Settings","n":"pnpm","b":"ignoreScripts Default: false Type: Boolean Do not execute any scripts defined in the project and its dependencies. :::note This flag does not prevent the execution of .pnpmfile.mjs ::: childConcurrency Default: 5 Type: Number The maximum number of child processes to allocate simultaneously to build node modules. sideEffectsCache Default: true Type: Boolean Use and cache the results of (pre/post)install hooks. When a pre/post install script modify the contents of a package (e.g. build output), pnpm saves the modified package in the global store. On future installs on the same machine, pnpm reus ignoreScripts childConcurrency sideEffectsCache sideEffectsCacheReadonly unsafePerm nodeOptions verifyDepsBeforeRun strictDepBuilds allowBuilds dangerouslyAllowAllBuilds","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/catalogmode.html","t":"Catalogmode","n":"pnpm","b":"catalogMode Added in: v10.12.1 Default: manual Type: manual , strict , prefer Controls if and how dependencies are added to the default catalog, when running . There are three modes: strict only allows dependency versions from the catalog. Adding a dependency outside the catalog's version range will cause an error. prefer prefers catalog versions, but will fall back to direct dependencies if no compatible version is found. manual (default) does not automatically add dependencies to the catalog. catalogMode","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/cleanupunusedcatalogs.html","t":"Cleanupunusedcatalogs","n":"pnpm","b":"cleanupUnusedCatalogs Added in: v10.15.0 Default: false Type: Boolean When set to , pnpm will remove unused catalog entries during installation. cleanupUnusedCatalogs","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/cli.html","t":"CLI & Node.js Settings","n":"pnpm","b":"CLI Settings [no ]color Default: auto Type: auto , always , never Controls colors in the output. auto output uses colors when the standard output is a terminal or TTY. always ignore the difference between terminals and pipes. You’ll rarely want this; in most scenarios, if you want color codes in your redirected output, you can instead pass a flag to the pnpm command to force it to use color codes. The default setting is almost always what you’ll want. never turns off colors. This is the setting used by . loglevel Default: info Type: debug , info , warn , error Any logs at or higher than the gi CLI Settings [no-]color loglevel useBetaCli recursiveInstall engineStrict npmPath pmOnFail ignoreWorkspaceRootCheck Node.js Settings nodeVersion runtimeOnFail nodeDownloadMirrors","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/cpuflag.html","t":"Cpuflag","n":"pnpm","b":"cpu=&lt;name\\ Added in: v10.14.0 Override CPU architecture of native modules to install. Acceptable values are same as field of , which comes from . --cpu=&lt;name\\>","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/dependency-resolution.html","t":"Dependency Resolution Settings","n":"pnpm","b":"overrides This field allows you to instruct pnpm to override any dependency in the dependency graph, including peer dependencies. This is useful for enforcing all your packages to use a single version of a dependency, backporting a fix, replacing a dependency with a fork, or removing an unused dependency. Note that the overrides field can only be set at the root of the project. An example of the field: You may specify the package the overridden dependency belongs to by separating the package selector from the dependency selector with a \" \", for example will only override the dependency of , no overrides Convergence overrides Overriding peer dependencies packageExtensions allowedDeprecatedVersions update update.ignoreDeps update.changeset update.githubActions update.githubActionsServer supportedArchitectures ignoredOptionalDependencies minimumReleaseAge minimumReleaseAgeExclude minimumReleaseAgeIgnoreMissingTime minimumReleaseAgeStrict trustPolicy trustPolicyExclude trustPolicyIgnoreAfter trustLockfile blockExoticSubdeps registries namedRegistries","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/enableprepostscripts.html","t":"Enableprepostscripts","n":"pnpm","b":"enablePrePostScripts Default: true Type: Boolean When , pnpm will run any pre/post scripts automatically. So running will be like running . enablePrePostScripts","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/findby.html","t":"Findby","n":"pnpm","b":"find by &lt;finder name\\ Added in: v10.16.0 Use a [finder function] defined in to match dependencies by properties other than name. [finder function]: ../finders.md --find-by &lt;finder_name\\>","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/libcflag.html","t":"Libcflag","n":"pnpm","b":"libc=&lt;name\\ Added in: v10.14.0 Override libc of native modules to install. Acceptable values are same as field of . --libc=&lt;name\\>","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/network.html","t":"Network & Request Settings","n":"pnpm","b":"Network Settings httpsProxy Default: null Type: url A proxy to use for outgoing HTTPS requests. If the , , or environment variables are set, their values will be used instead. If your proxy URL contains a username and password, make sure to URL encode them. For instance: Do not encode the colon ( ) between the username and password. httpProxy Default: null Type: url A proxy to use for outgoing HTTP requests. If the or environment variables are set, proxy settings will be honored by the underlying request library. noProxy Default: null Type: String A comma separated string of domain extensions  Network Settings httpsProxy httpProxy noProxy localAddress maxsockets strictSsl Request Settings gitShallowHosts networkConcurrency fetchRetries fetchRetryFactor fetchRetryMintimeout fetchRetryMaxtimeout fetchTimeout fetchWarnTimeoutMs fetchMinSpeedKiBps","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/node-modules.html","t":"Node-Modules & Hoisting Settings","n":"pnpm","b":"Node Modules Settings modulesDir Default: node modules Type: path The directory in which dependencies will be installed (instead of ). nodeLinker Default: isolated Type: isolated , hoisted , pnp Defines what linker should be used for installing Node packages. isolated dependencies are symlinked from a virtual store at . hoisted a flat without symlinks is created. Same as the created by npm or Yarn Classic. One of Yarn's libraries is used for hoisting, when this setting is used. Legitimate reasons to use this setting: 1. Your tooling doesn't work well with symlinks. A React Native project will  Node-Modules Settings modulesDir nodeLinker nodeExperimentalPackageMap nodePackageMapType symlink enableModulesDir virtualStoreDir virtualStoreDirMaxLength virtualStoreOnly packageImportMethod modulesCacheMaxAge dlxCacheMaxAge enableGlobalVirtualStore Dependency Hoisting Settings hoist hoistWorkspacePackages hoistPattern publicHoistPattern shamefullyHoist hoistingLimits","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/osflag.html","t":"Osflag","n":"pnpm","b":"os=&lt;name\\ Added in: v10.14.0 Override OS of native modules to install. Acceptable values are same as field of , which comes from . --os=&lt;name\\>","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/other.html","t":"Other Settings","n":"pnpm","b":"savePrefix Default: '^' Type: '^' , '~' , '' , '=' Configure how versions of packages installed to a file get prefixed. For example, if a package has version , by default its version is set to which allows minor upgrades for that package, but after it would be set to which only allows patch upgrades. Since v11.19.0, is also accepted: newly added dependencies are saved with an explicit operator ( ), which pins the exact version. keeps the operator when it updates such a pin. This setting is ignored when the added package has a range specified. For instance, will set the version of in to , regar savePrefix tag globalDir globalBinDir npmrcAuthFile stateDir cacheDir useStderr updateNotifier preferSymlinkedExecutables ignoreCompatibilityDb resolutionMode registrySupportsTimeField extendNodePath Why this is needed When to disable deployAllFiles dedupeDirectDeps optimisticRepeatInstall requiredScripts ci","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/peer-dependencies.html","t":"Peer Dependency Settings","n":"pnpm","b":"autoInstallPeers Default: true Type: Boolean When , any missing non optional peer dependencies are automatically installed. Version Conflicts If there are conflicting version requirements for a peer dependency from different packages, pnpm will not install any version of the conflicting peer dependency automatically. Instead, a warning is printed. For example, if one dependency requires and another requires , these requirements conflict, and no automatic installation will occur. Conflict Resolution In case of a version conflict, you'll need to evaluate which version of the peer dependency to i autoInstallPeers Version Conflicts Conflict Resolution dedupePeerDependents dedupePeers strictPeerDependencies resolvePeersFromWorkspaceRoot peerDependencyRules peerDependencyRules.ignoreMissing peerDependencyRules.allowedVersions peerDependencyRules.allowAny","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/scriptshell.html","t":"Scriptshell","n":"pnpm","b":"scriptShell Default: null Type: path The shell to use for scripts run with the command. For instance, to force usage of Git Bash on Windows: scriptShell","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/shellemulator.html","t":"Shellemulator","n":"pnpm","b":"shellEmulator Default: false Type: Boolean When , pnpm will use a JavaScript implementation of a [bash like shell] to execute scripts. This option simplifies cross platform scripting. For instance, by default, the next script will fail on non POSIX compliant systems: But if the setting is set to , it will work on all platforms. :::note Node.js 22 or higher supports running scripts without pnpm's assistance. For the example above, you can run the script with . However, the option has no effect on this. Scripts that depend on POSIX features are required to be run instead of to work in non POSIX  shellEmulator","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/store.html","t":"Store & Lockfile Settings","n":"pnpm","b":"Store Settings storeDir Default: If the $PNPM HOME env variable is set, then $PNPM HOME/store If the $XDG DATA HOME env variable is set, then $XDG DATA HOME/pnpm/store On Windows: ~/AppData/Local/pnpm/store On macOS: ~/Library/pnpm/store On Linux: ~/.local/share/pnpm/store Type: path The location where all the packages are saved on the disk. The store should be always on the same disk on which installation is happening, so there will be one store per disk. If there is a home directory on the current disk, then the store is created inside it. If there is no home on the disk, then the store is c Store Settings storeDir verifyStoreIntegrity useRunningStoreServer strictStorePkgContentCheck frozenStore Lockfile Settings lockfile preferFrozenLockfile lockfileIncludeTarballUrl gitBranchLockfile mergeGitBranchLockfilesBranchPattern peersSuffixMaxLength","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings/versioning.html","t":"Versioning Settings","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.13.0 These settings configure pnpm's native workspace release management, driven by and the bare . See Release management for the workflow they belong to. Where two workspace projects publish the same name, a project may be referenced by its prefixed workspace relative directory instead of its name in , , and the keys of . versioning.fixed Default: [] Type: string[][] Groups of packages that always release together at one shared version. The shared version is the highest current version in the group, bumped by the largest bump any member needs. A fixed group must move between lan versioning.fixed versioning.ignore versioning.maxBump versioning.lanes versioning.epics versioning.changelog.storage","s":"settings"},{"u":"settings.html","t":"Settings (pnpm-workspace.yaml)","n":"pnpm","b":"pnpm gets its configuration from the command line, environment variables, and . Only auth and registry settings are read from files. All other settings (like , , , etc.) must be configured in or the global . The command can be used to read and edit the contents of the project and global configuration files. The relevant configuration files are: Per project configuration file: Global configuration file :::note Authorization related settings are handled via . ::: Values in the configuration files may contain env variables using the syntax. The env variables may also be specified with default val packages packageConfigs Settings Dependency Resolution Node-Modules Settings Dependency Hoisting Settings Store Settings Lockfile Settings Network Settings Request Settings Peer Dependency Settings CLI Settings Node.js Settings Build Settings Versioning Settings Other Settings Workspace Settings Settings documented elsewhere"},{"u":"supply-chain-security.html","t":"Mitigating supply chain attacks","n":"pnpm","b":"Sometimes npm packages are compromised and published with malware. Luckily, there are companies like [Socket], [Snyk], [Xygeni] and [Aikido] that detect these compromised packages early. The npm registry usually removes the affected versions within hours. However, there is always a window of time between when the malware is published and when it is detected, during which you could be exposed. Fortunately, there are some things you can do with pnpm to minimize the risks. Block risky postinstall scripts Historically, most compromised packages have used scripts to run code immediately upon instal Block risky postinstall scripts Prevent exotic transitive dependencies Delay dependency updates Enforce trust with trustPolicy Use a lockfile"},{"u":"symlinked-node-modules-structure.html","t":"Symlinked nodemodules structure","n":"pnpm","b":":::info This article only describes how pnpm's are structured when there are no packages with peer dependencies. For the more complex scenario of dependencies with peers, see how peers are resolved. ::: pnpm's layout uses symbolic links to create a nested structure of dependencies. Every file of every package inside is a hard link to the content addressable store. Let's say you install that depends on . pnpm will hard link both packages to like this: These are the only \"real\" files in . Once all the packages are hard linked to , symbolic links are created to build the nested dependency graph s "},{"u":"typescript.html","t":"Working with TypeScript","n":"pnpm","b":"pnpm should work well with TypeScript out of the box most of the time. Do not preserve symlinks You should not use TypeScript with set to . TypeScript will not be able to resolve the type dependencies correctly in the linked . If you do need to preserve symlinks for some reason, then you should set pnpm's setting to . Workspace usage You might sometimes have issues if you have different versions of a dependency in a workspace. These issues happen when a package requires these types without having the type dependency in dependencies. For instance, if you have in your dependencies, which relies  Do not preserve symlinks Workspace usage"},{"u":"uninstall.html","t":"Uninstalling pnpm","n":"pnpm","b":"Removing the globally installed packages Before removing the pnpm CLI, it might make sense to remove all global packages that were installed by pnpm. To list all the global packages, run . There are two ways to remove the global packages: 1. Run with each global package listed. 2. Run to find the location of the global directory and remove it manually. Removing the pnpm CLI If you used the standalone script to install pnpm, then you should be able to uninstall the pnpm CLI by removing the pnpm home directory: You might also want to clean the env variable in your shell configuration file ( , or Removing the globally installed packages Removing the pnpm CLI Removing the global content-addressable store"},{"u":"using-changesets.html","t":"Using Changesets with pnpm","n":"pnpm","b":":::tip Since v11.13.0, pnpm can manage workspace releases natively, without the Changesets CLI. It reads and writes the same files. See Release management. Since v11.16.0, can also write a changeset for the dependency bumps an update makes. ::: :::note At the time of writing this documentation, the latest pnpm version was v10.4.1. The latest Changesets version was v2.28.0. ::: Setup To setup changesets on a pnpm workspace, install changesets as a dev dependency in the root of the workspace: Then run changesets' init command to generate a changesets config: Adding new changesets To generate a n Setup Adding new changesets Releasing changes Integration with GitHub Actions Add a publish script Add the workflow"},{"u":"versioning.html","t":"Release management","n":"pnpm","b":"Added in: v11.13.0 pnpm can version and release a workspace on its own, without a separate release tool. The workflow has two halves: 1. As you work, records change intents — small markdown files in saying which packages a change affects, how each should be bumped, and a summary that becomes the changelog entry. These are committed alongside the change. 2. At release time, the bare consumes the pending intents: it bumps versions across the workspace, propagates to dependents, writes changelogs, and records what it consumed in a committed ledger. Intent files use the changesets format, so an ex Recording a change Releasing First releases Configuration Fixed groups Lanes Epics Changelogs The ledger"},{"u":"workspaces.html","t":"Workspace","n":"pnpm","b":"pnpm has built in support for monorepositories (AKA multi package repositories, multi project repositories, or monolithic repositories). You can create a workspace to unite multiple projects inside a single repository. A workspace must have a [ ] file in its root. [ ]: settings.md :::tip If you are looking into monorepo management, you might also want to look into [Bit]. Bit uses pnpm under the hood but automates a lot of the things that are currently done manually in a traditional workspace managed by pnpm/npm/Yarn. There's an article about that talks about it: [Painless Monorepo Dependency M Workspace protocol (workspace:) Referencing workspace packages through aliases Referencing workspace packages through their relative path Publishing workspace packages Release workflow Troubleshooting Usage examples Configuration linkWorkspacePackages injectWorkspacePackages dedupeInjectedDeps syncInjectedDepsAfterScripts preferWorkspacePackages sharedWorkspaceLockfile saveWorkspaceProtocol includeWorkspaceRoot ignoreWorkspaceCycles disallowWorkspaceCycles failIfNoMatch"}];
